<SiteLayout title="Module 14: Transport Layer — fred.fish" description="The transport layer provides communication between application processes. TCP and UDP use port numbers to identify services, but they offer different reliability and overhead trade-offs." active="cisco">
<section class="source-lesson">
<div class="lesson-nav"><a href="/cisco/learning/semester-1/">← Semester 1 course</a><a href="/cisco/practice/">Adaptive practice ↗</a></div>
<header class="lesson-hero"><Eyebrow index="S1 / 14" label="MODULE 14 / TCP AND UDP" /><h1>Connect applications.<br /><span>Deliver the data.</span></h1><p>The transport layer provides communication between application processes. TCP and UDP use port numbers to identify services, but they offer different reliability and overhead trade-offs.</p></header>
<section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">01</div><div><h2>Understand transport-layer roles</h2><p>Transport protocols segment application data and identify the sending and receiving applications with port numbers. They support multiplexing: many application conversations can share the same host IP address. TCP and UDP are the primary transport protocols in the TCP/IP suite.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE IMAGES</span><code>IMG-1134 · IMG-1125</code></div></div></section>
<section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">02</div><div><h2>Compare TCP and UDP</h2><p>TCP is connection-oriented and provides ordered, reliable byte-stream delivery using sequence numbers, acknowledgments, retransmissions, and flow control. UDP is connectionless and has a smaller header and less overhead, but does not provide built-in delivery, ordering, or retransmission guarantees. Applications choose the service that fits their needs.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE IMAGES</span><code>IMG-1272 · IMG-1259 · IMG-1261 · IMG-1319 · IMG-1305</code></div></div></section>
<section class="lesson-section"><div class="lesson-number">03</div><div><h2>Use ports and reliability mechanisms</h2><p>Port numbers identify application endpoints. Common examples include DNS 53, SSH 22, HTTP 80, and HTTPS 443. TCP sequence numbers identify bytes in the stream; acknowledgments identify the next byte expected. The three-way handshake uses SYN, SYN-ACK, and ACK to establish a TCP connection.</p><div class="lesson-source"><span>SOURCE IMAGES</span><code>IMG-1168 · IMG-1322</code></div></div></section>
<section class="lesson-end"><div><Eyebrow label="CHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING" /><h2>Transport Layer</h2><p>Practice the key terms, procedures, and troubleshooting decisions from this module.</p></div><a href="/cisco/practice/">Start practice →</a></section>
</section>
</SiteLayout>
